      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Beyond the \"common good\" slogan, rigorous social democratic theory is often grounded in the **Capability Approach** (fleshed out by theorists like Amartya Sen and often embraced by democratic socialists) and **social contract theory** (revisited by thinkers like John Rawls).\n\nThe \"rigorous\" argument posits that true **liberty is not merely the absence of interference** (negative liberty), but the **substantive freedom** to actually pursue one's own life plan (positive liberty). Without state-ensured \"liberty basics\" and equity, the \"free\" poor have no real choice in the only option available to them (survival or exploitation), making the \"right to liberty\" of the wealthy both illusory for them as well as unjust in the aggregate.\n\nTherefore, taxation is not seen as a \"theft\" of the abundant but as the **necessary moral realization of the \"differing end\" of the social contract**: the state has a legitimate moral claim on the surplus of the rich to correct \"arbitrary\" natural advantages (like lottery birth) and ensure the \"effective freedom\" of all citizens is upheld.",
